    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d640">

                <pb facs="1319" n="1307" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg1307"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Marshall to
                        Mr. Walter S. Robertson, at
                    Peiping</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-07-06">6 July 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1062. Have contacted Government officials regarding your 4847<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d640-f1"/> and have found after several lengthy discussions that the
                    Government has not, in spite of the Generalissimo’s assurances to me to the
                    contrary, issued orders to National field commanders subsequent to 7 June
                    relative to aggressive military actions. I was assured however that the orders
                    issued applied to field commanders in North and Central China as well as those
                    commanders in Manchuria. Contact with the Communist representatives indicates
                    much the same status except that their initial orders on 7 June were to
                    Manchurian field commanders with information copies to commanders in China
                    proper. I have however gotten commitment out of both sides to press this matter
                    to issue new orders and should have that information available soon. I will keep
                    you advised.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
